From 43bf0c884db1f87d749f35015e813a6fa886bcb6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexander Penev <alexander.p.penev@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 10:56:41 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] core: MixedEval<Lo,Hi> - and the measurement that says
 what it is for

Two types over one geometry: a cheap one that may reject, an exact one that
is the only one allowed to answer. lo() never reports a distance; hi() is
the reported value; refine() decides where the exact type is worth paying
for; wideCoverage() reports whether hi() is really Hi, because a tree with
one unconverted node evaluated "in double" is worse than one evaluated
entirely in float - the error is real and in an unknown place.

TWO THINGS THE TESTS CAUGHT, BOTH WORTH MORE THAN THE CLASS.

1. A SAMPLED DELTA IS NOT A BOUND. The first version called the accessors
   loBound/upBound and asserted the band held on fresh points. It does not:
   delta 6.3e-04 mm from 20000 samples, then 2 of 50000 fresh points outside
   it, the worst by 1% of delta. Small, not zero, and that is the whole
   difference between an estimate and a proof. Renamed loEstimate/upEstimate,
   documented for what they are, and pointed at node_interval.hpp for a
   rejection that has to be sound. The test now asserts the rate and the
   size of the overshoot, which are true, instead of zero, which is not.

2. THE CHEAP TYPE IS NOT CHEAP. Scalar f32 evaluation is SLOWER than f64 on
   every node kind - sphere 1.34x, box 1.43x, tube 1.13x, cone 1.48x, poly
   1.67x, frame 1.78x - and on a real sphere trace the mixed path never beat
   plain f64 at any tolerance (27.3 vs 29.8 ns/step at 1 mm, 22.4 vs 26.1 at
   1e-3). Structural, not incidental: parameters are stored as double, so
   the f32 path converts every one on the way in and scalar SSE returns
   nothing for it - no extra lanes, no less traffic.

   Measured on random points in a box, refine() paid for f64 on 0.1% of
   queries and it looked free. On the queries a march actually makes it is
   7.7%, and still no faster. That is the fourth time in this project that a
   benchmark on a convenient distribution disagreed with the real one.

So Lo = float buys accuracy control on the CPU, not speed: hi() is the only
reportable distance and refine() says where it is needed. Where f32 should
pay is GPU and SIMD, where it doubles the lane count - not measured here and
therefore not claimed. The CPU speed in this area came from the data
structure, not the type.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01B5ALyYbzrXTGtc7LqRPGCz
